ime 59304 within the inspected interval; with half-windows of 5000 this yields the before/after windows [54304,59303] and [59304,64304]. Per-node degrees (steps 06/07) and explicit neighbour lists for representative nodes (114: steps 08/09; 182: steps 10/11) show largely disjoint contact sets before vs after the change point, consistent with an abrupt replacement of partners rather than gradual turnover. Global activity and node popularity were checked (steps 02/03 and 04/05) and do not explain the sudden shift, and the 1-hop static subgraph (step_12) confirms that static connectivity alone would not reveal the temporal replacement. Together these temporal patterns support a regime-switch (abrupt change) for the group in [45074,75074]."}, "raw_final_response": "{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_107b45e5\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [114, 140, 182, 293, 356, 654, 731, 959, 1107, 1260, 1268, 1311],\n  \"window\": [45074, 75074],\n  \"confidence\": 0.90,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The neighborhood_evolution series (step_01) shows a sharp, full change (peak_change = 1.0) at time 59304 within the inspected interval; with half-windows of 5000 this yields the before/after windows [54304,59303] and [59304,64304]. Per-node degrees (steps 06/07) and explicit neighbour lists for representative nodes (114: steps 08/09; 182: steps 10/11) show largely disjoint contact sets before vs after the change point, consistent with an abrupt replacement of partners rather than gradual turnover. Global activity and node popularity were checked (steps 02/03 and 04/05) and do not explain the sudden shift, and the 1-hop static subgraph (step_12) confirms that static connectivity alone would not reveal the temporal replacement. Together these temporal patterns support a regime-switch (abrupt change) for the group in [45074,75074].\"\n}", "total_input_tokens": 45277, "total_output_tokens": 4795, "total_tokens": 50072, "total_latency_ms": 74330, "llm_calls": 13, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}